      "title": "PROMPTING - read before your first render",
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "## Prompting H3 - the audio is half the model\n\nH3 generates **video and stereo audio in one pass**. A one-line Stable-Diffusion-style\nprompt will give you mediocre audio and you will conclude the model is overhyped. Give it\nstructure. This is the exact prompt used for every measurement in this pack:\n\n```\nCinematic night footage, 35mm, shallow depth of field, wet tarmac, warm streetlight,\nnatural grain, no lettering anywhere in frame.\n\nScene overview: A white sedan rolls slowly along a wet European street at night.\n\nStoryboard:\n[0s-5s] One continuous take. The car moves steadily through frame as light travels\nacross the paint. No cuts.\n\nCamera: Static low tracking shot at bumper height, slight handheld drift.\n\nAudio: a deep V8 engine rolling past at low speed, tyres on wet tarmac, faint street\nambience.\n\nMusic: none\n\nNo text, subtitles, lettering, signage, logos or watermarks of any kind.\n```\n\n**Rules learned the hard way (measured over ~40 renders):**\n- **ONE CONTINUOUS TAKE.** Writing \"Shot 1 / Shot 2\" makes H3 genuinely cut.\n- **Name subjects specifically.** It anchors on the most specific noun phrase and fills the\n  rest by association. \"Large white American sedan\" produced a 1960s classic; naming the\n  exact model and its features produced the right car every time. **Partial specificity is\n  worse than none** - it looks like it worked.\n- **Speech must be written out verbatim** or you get gibberish. And judge audio only at\n  10+ steps: below ~6 steps speech breaks before the picture does.\n- **Setting gets overridden** more readily than subject, lighting or camera. If a location\n  is non-negotiable, lead with it.\n- **Negatives about CONTENT are unreliable** (\"no visible faces\" was ignored repeatedly).\n  Negatives about *lettering* do hold.\n\nFrame count: duration is snapped by the maths node to the model's grid - frames must satisfy\n`n % 17 == 5`, so 5.0s becomes 124 frames, not 120. That is why the expression looks odd."
      ],
      "color": "#3a2a4a",
      "bgcolor": "#2a1e33"
